The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (q->skb) {
    dev_kfree_skb(q->skb);
    q->skb = NULL;
} else {
    page_pool_put_full_page(q->page_pool, page, true);
}
Reason:
When an error occurs on a subsequent non-linear buffer (i.e., `q->skb` is not NULL because we are building a multi-fragment skb), the code enters the `if (q->skb)` branch. It frees the skb but does **not** call `page_pool_put_full_page()` for the current page fragment. This page fragment was already allocated from the page pool but is never linked to the skb, so it is lost, causing a memory leak. The `page_pool_put_full_page()` is only called in the `else` branch (when `q->skb` is NULL, i.e., on first buffer errors), leaving a gap for the leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (q->skb) {
    dev_kfree_skb(q->skb);
    q->skb = NULL;
}
page_pool_put_full_page(q->page_pool, page, true);
Reason:
By moving `page_pool_put_full_page()` outside the conditional, it is called unconditionally on every error path. This ensures that regardless of whether an skb is being built (`q->skb` set) or not, the current page fragment is always returned to the page pool, preventing the memory leak. This effectively reverts the problematic conditional introduced by the previous commit and guarantees proper page recycling.

